  "response": "Attributing a bridge failure to design or to execution is a problem of reconciling the physical record of the structure with the paper record of the decisions made about it, and the Florida International University walkway offers a model of how the two are brought together. When the pedestrian bridge collapsed onto an open roadway during construction, the gravity of the event led the National Transportation Safety Board to take charge of the federal investigation. The investigators' first obligation was to establish precisely what the structure was doing in the period leading up to the failure. Structural cracking was documented at the north end of the span in the days before the collapse, and workers were re-tensioning post-tensioning rods in a diagonal member at the moment the span fell; cracking already recorded at the north end rules out re-tensioning work being underway in that diagonal as the structure came down.\n\nOut of that record two candidate explanations arise, and at least one of them is true, perhaps both: the contractor left the span unshored during the re-tensioning operation; the design drawings understated the shear demand at the critical truss node. The federal inquiry so constituted led directly to the principal technical exercise of the case. Forensic engineers rebuilt the structural model using finite element analysis to recompute member capacities. Tensile testing of recovered reinforcing steel showed that it met the specified yield strength. Those recomputed capacities confirm the understatement of shear demand in the drawings. By contrast with the reinforcing steel, the concrete as placed was deficient: concrete cylinder tests showed compressive strengths forty percent below specification.\n\nThe documentary and regulatory record runs parallel to this laboratory work. The Occupational Safety and Health Administration issued citations to the construction contractor after the collapse. The peer reviewer's scope of work omitted a capacity check of the critical nodal region. The citations issued against the contractor show that the span was indeed left without shoring while the rods were being re-tensioned.\n\nOnly against an assembled record of this kind can a probable cause be stated, and the competing versions must then be measured against one another. The probable cause was overloading of the deck by parked construction equipment. The probable cause of the collapse was errors in the design firm's load and capacity calculations; overloading by parked equipment and miscalculation of loads and capacities cannot both have brought the span down. That is to say, the failure is attributable to the engineer of record miscalculating structural demand and resistance. The gap left in the peer reviewer's scope indicates exactly that attribution, since the one independent check capable of catching the error at the node was never carried out. Where the design firm and the contractor each point at the other, it is this interlocking of recomputed mechanics, material testing and the audit trail of review that decides between them.",
